\description{
Inverts the \code{referenced_works} column of the works corpus into an edge set
keyed by the \strong{cited} work, so that "which works cite X?" becomes a point
lookup instead of a full-corpus scan. This is what makes \code{\link[=get_citing]{get_citing()}}
possible offline: the snapshot's \code{cited_by_api_url} is a URL and useless
without network access, but the citing direction is recoverable by
inverting the references every work already carries.
}
\details{
\code{\link[=get_cited]{get_cited()}} does \strong{not} need this index -- the works a paper references
are simply its own \code{referenced_works} -- so only one index is built, not a
pair.

Each part file holds:
\describe{
\item{cited_id}{The cited work's OpenAlex ID, long form \code{VARCHAR}}
\item{citing_id}{The citing work's OpenAlex ID, long form \code{VARCHAR}}
}
sorted by \verb{(cited_id, citing_id)}, matching the long-form convention of
\verb{<dataset>_id_idx/} and \verb{<dataset>_doi_idx.parquet} so every index in the
family stores IDs the same way. Note this costs real space at three
billion rows: \code{citing_id} is effectively random within a block and so does
not dictionary-compress, unlike the sorted \code{cited_id}.
}
\section{Layout}{

Like \verb{<dataset>_id_idx/}, this index is a \strong{hive-partitioned directory}
(only \verb{<dataset>_doi_idx.parquet} remains a single file):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{works_cite_idx/
  _index_meta.parquet
  cited_block=0/part-0.parquet
  cited_block=1/part-0.parquet
  ...
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The reason is scale. The full OpenAlex works corpus holds roughly three
billion reference edges; a single sorted file would need a global sort of
that many rows, and its footer alone would cost tens of megabytes to parse
on every query. Partitioning by a coarse block of the cited ID keeps each
part small enough to sort in memory and lets a lookup open only the handful
of parts it needs.

\verb{_index_meta.parquet} is written \strong{last}; its presence is what marks the
index complete, and it records the \code{block_size} the query side must use.
}

